1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,407
Christmas is not an emergency.

2
00:00:02,827 --> 00:00:05,815
It happens on December twenty-fifth. Every single year.

3
00:00:06,235 --> 00:00:12,401
And most of the expenses that wreck a budget work the same way. They're not surprises. They're just not monthly.

4
00:00:13,171 --> 00:00:17,841
The fix is called a sinking fund. Here's exactly how it works, with real numbers.

5
00:00:18,611 --> 00:00:19,731
First, the problem.

6
00:00:20,151 --> 00:00:24,623
Remember the example from episode one? Here's when each cost actually lands.

7
00:00:24,783 --> 00:00:28,009
Annual subscriptions, $120, in January.

8
00:00:28,169 --> 00:00:30,910
Medical copays, $300, spread through the year.

9
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:33,250
Registration, $180, in April.

10
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:36,496
Car repairs, $500, whenever the car decides.

11
00:00:36,656 --> 00:00:39,041
And the holidays, $600, in December.

12
00:00:39,461 --> 00:00:47,045
On a calendar it looks like this. Some months, nothing. Then July brings a $500 repair, and December wants $600.

13
00:00:47,465 --> 00:00:53,174
But averaged out, it's about $142 a month. Or $66 a paycheck.

14
00:00:53,944 --> 00:00:58,537
Now, here's why paying before beats paying after, even with the exact same money.

15
00:00:58,957 --> 00:01:06,614
Option one: let the costs land on a credit card at 24%, then pay $66 a paycheck toward it afterward.

16
00:01:07,034 --> 00:01:16,390
Interest builds while you catch up, so you slowly fall behind. After five years you owe $784, and the interest grows every year.

17
00:01:16,810 --> 00:01:24,083
Option two: move the same $66 into savings first. When the bill shows up, the money's already there. You owe nothing.

18
00:01:24,503 --> 00:01:26,330
Same money. Opposite results.

19
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,375
So here's how to set them up, in three steps.

20
00:01:29,795 --> 00:01:36,606
Step one. Look backwards. Open last year's bank and card statements, and write down every cost that wasn't monthly.

21
00:01:37,026 --> 00:01:44,508
Holidays, birthdays, car stuff, annual renewals, school costs, the vet. If it happened last year, it'll probably happen again.

22
00:01:44,928 --> 00:01:51,334
Step two. Split them into two kinds. Deadline funds have a date, like car registration, due in April.

23
00:01:51,754 --> 00:01:58,918
Starting in January, that's 7 paychecks away. $180 divided by 7 is $26 a paycheck.

24
00:01:59,338 --> 00:02:05,765
The holidays? Start in January, and $600 over 24 paychecks is $25 each.

25
00:02:06,185 --> 00:02:13,314
Someday funds don't have a date. Car repairs, medical bills. Give each a target, like $500 and $300.

26
00:02:13,734 --> 00:02:16,855
Fill them a little each paycheck. When you use one, refill it.

27
00:02:17,275 --> 00:02:24,032
Step three. Automate it. Set a transfer to savings on the same day your paycheck lands, so you never have to decide.

28
00:02:24,452 --> 00:02:31,739
One savings account is fine. Just track each fund's balance, or use a bank that lets you split savings into named buckets.

29
00:02:32,159 --> 00:02:32,766
Recap.

30
00:02:32,926 --> 00:02:34,737
List every cost that isn't monthly.

31
00:02:34,897 --> 00:02:38,092
Deadline funds: divide the cost by the paychecks left.

32
00:02:38,252 --> 00:02:40,780
Someday funds: fill to a target, and refill.

33
00:02:40,940 --> 00:02:42,289
And automate it on payday.

34
00:02:43,059 --> 00:02:47,974
The Paycheck Planner in the description has a surprise fund tab that does this math for you.

35
00:02:48,394 --> 00:02:53,260
Subscribe, and next time we'll build a holiday budget that doesn't follow you into March.
